[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, let me recall what a selective ultrafilter is. A selective ultrafilter [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(also called Ramsey ultrafilter) is an ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on $\omega$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msuch that for every function $f: \omega \to \omega$, there exists a set $A \in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{U}$ such that $f|_A$ is either constant or injective.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mEquivalently, for every partition of $\omega$ into small sets (sets not in $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{U}$), there is a big set that meets each piece in at most one point. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThis is the "selectivity" property: given any partition $\omega = \coprod_{j} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mP_j$ with $P_j \notin \mathcal{U}$, there exists $A \in \mathcal{U}$ such that [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$|A \cap P_j| \leq 1$ for all $j$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the question is about a "simultaneous" version of this. We have a partition[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$\omega = \coprod_i S_i$ into small infinite sets, and we want to choose [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbijections $\varphi_i: \omega \to S_i$ such that the following "lifting" [0m
